  "title": "Kourovka Notebook Problem 21.86",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "A group $G$ is said to be sofic if for every finite set $F\\subseteq G$ containing $1$ and every $\\varepsilon>0$ there exist $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and a map $\\phi:F\\to S_n$ such that $\\phi(1)=1$, $d(\\phi(gh),\\phi(g)\\phi(h))<\\varepsilon$ for all $g,h$ such that $gh\\in F$, and $\\phi(g)$ does not have fixed points for every $g\\in F\\setminus\\{1\\}$. Is every group sofic?",
    "sha256": "120b28087790e404b7b3ac729019115022d1e0e3ee8d4b8e1b55b069cde1853b",
